---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import households from "../data/households.json";
import { findPerson } from "../lib/people.js";
import HouseholdTree from "../components/HouseholdTree.astro";
---
<Base title="The households">
  <h1>The households</h1>
  <p class="lede">{households.length} reconstructed families of the parish of Sant'Andrea at Arienzo and
  of the civil registers after it — read entry by entry. Every name below is a link to that person.</p>

  {households.map(h => (
    <section style="margin:2.4rem 0">
      <h2 style="margin-top:1rem">{h.name}</h2>
      <HouseholdTree household={h} showHead={false} />
      <div class="scroll"><table>
        <thead><tr><th>Person</th><th>Event</th><th>Date</th><th>What the register says</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>{h.members.map(m => (
          <tr>
            <td><strong><a href={u("/people/" + (findPerson(m.person, h.name)||{}).slug)}>{m.person}</a></strong><br/><span class="small">{m.role}</span></td>
            <td>{m.event}</td>
            <td class="mono">{m.date}</td>
            <td><em>{m.evidence}</em>{m.ark ? <><br/><span class="mono small">{m.ark}</span></> : null}</td>
          </tr>))}</tbody>
      </table></div>
    </section>
  ))}

  <h2>What these tables establish</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The line reaches a generation further back than it did.</strong> Pasquale Falco's
    baptism of 24 October 1767 names his father <strong>Matteo Falco</strong> and his mother
    <strong>Francesca Crisci</strong>. Matteo replaces the unproven Agostino Falcone.</li>
    <li><strong>Vincenzo's marriage names four people at once</strong> — himself, his father Pasquale,
    his mother Chiara Rivetti, and his wife Andreana's father Arcangelo Crisci. It is the single most
    load-bearing document found.</li>
    <li><strong>Matteo's other son is traceable too.</strong> Giuseppe Falco married Antonia di Guida,
    and their household can be followed for three more decades — including a son named
    <strong>Matteo after his grandfather</strong>, who died in 1809.</li>
    <li><strong>The Crisci marry in three times.</strong> Matteo married a Crisci in the 1760s; his
    grandson Vincenzo married Andreana Crisci two generations later; and Pasquale's daughter Anna
    married an Arcangelo Crisci and died at 22 in 1820.</li>
    <li><strong>One marriage needed the Pope.</strong> Crescenzo Falco, son of Giuseppe and Antonia di
    Guida, married Angela Maria Morgillo only <em>medio dispensatione obtenta a Sanctissimo Domino
    Papa nostro Gregorio super quarto gradu consanguinitatis</em> — by dispensation from Gregory XVI
    for kinship in the fourth degree. The couple were third cousins, and the parish knew it.</li>
    <li><strong>And one Falco married a Falco.</strong> Francesco Falco and Raffaela Falco buried their
    son Clemente, aged five, in August 1855.</li>
    <li><strong>Children die.</strong> Maria Angela, baptised February 1802, was buried in March 1805
    <em>aetatis suae annorum 3 circiter</em>. Giovanna was buried the same year. Giuseppe, son of
    Crescenzo, lived a few days. The name Maria was given twice, in 1789 and again in 1790.</li>
    <li><strong>The same women recur.</strong> <em>Cecilia Martone</em> appears as midwife or godmother
    across some twenty years of these registers; <em>Gelsumina Ruggiero</em> stands godmother to
    Vincenzo's children. A parish is a small number of people doing the same offices for one another.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Before Matteo</h2>
  <p>The line proper begins with Matteo Falco in the 1760s. But Falcos are at Arienzo long before that,
  and one early household has something none of the others do — <strong>a stated place of origin</strong>:</p>
  <blockquote>
    Anno Domini 1714 … infanti nato ex <strong>Francisco Falco <em>Casalis Paulisii</em></strong>
    et <strong>Antonia Loffreda filia Dominici</strong> … imposui nomen <strong>Joseph</strong>.
  </blockquote>
  <p><em>Casalis Paulisii</em> — of the hamlet of <strong>Paolisi</strong>, the next comune east and
  over the provincial border into Benevento. <strong>Francesco Falco was an incomer.</strong> He and
  Antonia Loffreda baptised three children at Sant'Andrea: Giuseppe in July 1714, a son on
  20 September 1717 whose name the machine reads only as <em>"Mart…es"</em>, and Domenico in
  February 1726.</p>
  <p><strong>Is he Matteo's ancestor?</strong> The dates allow it — Matteo was an adult by 1759, so he
  could be Francesco's grandson, or just possibly the son of 1717. <strong>No document joins them</strong>,
  and this archive will not join them for the sake of a tidy line.</p>
  <p>Further back still: a <strong>Tommaso Falco</strong> and Marta Mara baptised a daughter Lucretia at
  <strong>San Felice a Cancello in 1637</strong> — the entry written in Italian, not Latin. And an index
  page of 1654 already lists Falcos among Crisci, Morgillo, Martone, Rivetti, Carfora, de Lucia and
  Porrino: the whole in-law network was in place before the Restoration.</p>
  <p class="small">A footnote with a long echo — in 1896 a Pasquale Falco of Arienzo, a
  <em>carrettiere</em>, married Mariangela Attanasi <strong>born at Paolisi</strong>. The traffic ran
  both ways for two hundred years.</p>

  <h2>Where it stops</h2>
  <p><strong>Matteo Falco's marriage to Francesca Crisci</strong> — which would name his own father and
  settle whether Francesco of Paolisi belongs above him — should fall in the Arienzo marriage register
  between about 1760 and 1766. It has been searched for across 2,056 transcribed marriage and death
  pages and <strong>not found</strong>: either it is not among the transcribed leaves, or it is not
  filmed. His baptism would fall in the gap the baptism register leaves between
  <strong>1738 and 1761</strong>.</p>
</Base>
